1
00:00:00,025 --> 00:00:04,092
I'm excited to invite you into a very, very special place.

2
00:00:04,092 --> 00:00:06,903
This is Hatchards in London's Piccadilly.

3
00:00:06,903 --> 00:00:11,457
It's one of the oldest book shops in our capital, built in 1797.

4
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:19,378
As part of the Dior Book Tote Club, I'd like to welcome you inside.

5
00:00:19,378 --> 00:00:24,677
You come into a bookshop and you just see everybody's interest, you just follow people's minds.

6
00:00:24,677 --> 00:00:33,135
All the little whims that go off in all of our brains, followed to the ultimate degree by these authors, and it blows my mind really.

7
00:00:33,135 --> 00:00:36,430
[《On The Road》] This is Jack Kerouac's "On the Road."

8
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:49,107
I was on a road trip with my father and we went to the west coast of Scotland which is remote and wild and the house that we were staying in had a very interesting collection, small collection, of books.

9
00:00:49,107 --> 00:00:50,279
And one of them was this one.

10
00:00:50,279 --> 00:00:55,860
It's travel, it's sex, it's drugs, and maybe it was way too adult for a 14 year old in fact it definitely was.

11
00:00:55,860 --> 00:01:02,056
So, am I ready to see if this book still stirs me like it did when I was 14?

12
00:01:02,056 --> 00:01:03,086
I think I am.

13
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:08,046
I'm going to re-read it.

14
00:01:08,046 --> 00:01:09,590
[《Slouching Towards Bethlehem》] Here's my second book.

15
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:20,287
I wanted to talk about "Slouching Towards Bethlehem", which is a collection of essays by Joan Didion, who is one of the most concise, pertinent observers of people.

16
00:01:20,287 --> 00:01:25,440
One of the essays in this collection that I love is her essay on self-respect.

17
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:34,073
She says: "Although to be driven back upon oneself is an uneasy affair at best, rather like trying to cross a border with borrowed credentials."

18
00:01:34,073 --> 00:01:35,448
Nice.

19
00:01:35,448 --> 00:01:41,103
"It seems to me now the one condition necessary to the beginnings of real self-respect."&nbsp;

20
00:01:41,103 --> 00:01:47,211
Most of our platitudes notwithstanding, self-deception remains the most difficult deception. "

21
00:01:47,211 --> 00:01:55,427
The tricks that work on others count for nothing in that very well-lit back alley where one keeps one's assignations with oneself. "

22
00:01:55,427 --> 00:01:58,689
It's always worth spending time with Joan Didion.

23
00:01:58,689 --> 00:02:07,238
She was curious, and I think curiosity is where freedom lies and interest lies.

24
00:02:07,238 --> 00:02:13,020
[《A Little Life》] The third book that I'm going to put in my Book Tote: "A Little Life" by Hanya Yanagihara.

25
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:19,057
This is truly one of the most brilliant, devastating, magnificent novels I have ever read.

26
00:02:19,057 --> 00:02:20,891
It's a book that will make you cry.

27
00:02:20,891 --> 00:02:22,430
I don't know how she wrote this book.

28
00:02:22,430 --> 00:02:26,101
It's one of those books where you think "How did this come out of you?

29
00:02:26,101 --> 00:02:30,355
How have you made these people we care about so blisteringly?

30
00:02:30,355 --> 00:02:31,454
How have you done this?" "

31
00:02:31,454 --> 00:02:34,364
It's a book I didn't want to leave behind.

32
00:02:34,364 --> 00:02:36,964
I'm going to slip it into my bag.

33
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:40,321
[Children Section] Oh, we're coming to the children's section.

34
00:02:40,321 --> 00:02:43,291
Oh, we didn't even talk about children's books.

35
00:02:43,291 --> 00:02:47,200
But there are so many here that I would put in my tote.

36
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:49,614
"The Apple is Everything" — that's a book for me.&nbsp;

37
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:54,385
One of my favorite things in life are apples.

38
00:02:54,385 --> 00:02:59,490
I love all their complex shapes and tastes.

39
00:02:59,490 --> 00:03:06,818
Well, I've just learnt something that, erm, in French "Tomber dans les pommes" is to faint, which is a very nice expression.

40
00:03:06,818 --> 00:03:15,099
I need this book in my life, I'm afraid.

41
00:03:15,099 --> 00:03:18,805
[Thomas Hardy's] Whenever I'm missing England, I read Thomas Hardy.

42
00:03:18,805 --> 00:03:20,587
That is "The Mayor of Casterbridge".

43
00:03:20,587 --> 00:03:24,715
This is a terrible story about a man who sells his wife.

44
00:03:24,715 --> 00:03:28,105
It's a moment of total irrational hubris.

45
00:03:28,105 --> 00:03:32,097
I think I've never been interested in speed reading because I like the sound of the words.

46
00:03:32,097 --> 00:03:38,966
I don't want to just read for information, because I think part of the meaning is in how words work rhythmically.

47
00:03:38,966 --> 00:03:50,303
[Closing Part] You know, the other really magical thing about the Dior Book Tote Club is that we are shooting in a bookshop after closing, which has a very special feel about it.

48
00:03:50,303 --> 00:03:52,996
Because there's nobody here.

49
00:03:52,996 --> 00:03:57,310
I've only recently discovered how brilliant graphic novels are.

50
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:03,708
It's about women in their 50s falling out of love with their husbands, going dancing, meeting men.

51
00:04:03,708 --> 00:04:09,818
It's about sex and work and frustrations, and it's just not what you would expect at all.

52
00:04:09,818 --> 00:04:13,302
Books move you, but it's already really wonderful when books make you laugh.

53
00:04:13,302 --> 00:04:18,324
It would also be a good present for my mum, on Mother's Day.

54
00:04:18,324 --> 00:04:24,569
A wonderful thing, of course, in a bookshop you can travel the world while sitting still.

55
00:04:24,569 --> 00:04:27,069
And I know that in here is an invitation to spending time in some interesting company.

